runs(p, "**Abstract.** URL-based phishing detectors report miss rates close to zero, but it is rarely clear what those rates rest on. We built a four-layer detector: blocklists, typosquatting matching, a BiLSTM score fed to XGBoost with a local Tranco rank, and an LLM that reads sanitized page content. Layers B and C form a local gate that takes about 33 ms and misses 0.03 % of phishing on a held-out test set, 0.43 % on PhreshPhish and 0.56 % on a Vietnamese set that shares no domain with training, while clearing 44 to 58 % of clean URLs. Stratifying these results shows that the gate acts mostly as a popularity check: over 99 % of the clean URLs it clears are on Tranco-ranked domains, and 68 of its 70 misses are too. On the 67 Vietnamese phishing URLs hosted on ranked domains it misses 22.4 %. The LLM stage behaves very differently across sets. It called 16.6 % of live Vietnamese phishing pages safe, taken from a list dated 2.5 years earlier, but none of 149 pages from a same-day OpenPhish feed. Re-registration of the domains does not explain this gap. End to end, 0 of 207 fresh phishing URLs were called safe (95 % CI upper bound 1.8 %) with Layer A bypassed, against 9.3 % on the old Vietnamese list.", 9)
p = doc.add_paragraph(); p.alignment = WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.LEFT
p.paragraph_format.left_indent = Cm(0.6); p.paragraph_format.right_indent = Cm(0.6); p.paragraph_format.space_after = Pt(6)
runs(p, "**Keywords:** Phishing detection · URL classification · Large language models · Prompt injection · Evaluation bias", 9)

H1("1   Introduction")
P("A phishing check has to answer at the moment of a click, and the two usual tools both struggle there. Blocklists only work after a site has been reported, and their coverage has been measured as incomplete {c:phishtime}. An LLM can read what a page is trying to do {c:chatphish}, but calling one on every click adds seconds and cost, and it exposes the model to instructions planted in the page {c:greshake}. Local coverage adds a further gap: in our data, three global feeds caught only 0.5 % of a Vietnamese phishing list.")
P("A common answer is a cascade: cheap URL checks first, and the LLM only for what they cannot clear. Such a cascade is judged by its miss rate, and the miss rates in this field are very low. Our own gate reaches 0.031 % on its test split. We wanted to know what that number means. A miss rate depends on which phishing URLs the set contains, and a model given a popularity rank may learn little else. So this paper reports the system and then tries to break its headline results.")
P("We ask four questions:")
L(["**RQ1.** How much clean traffic can a local gate clear with phishing misses near zero, and does this hold on other sources, languages and dates?",
   "**RQ2.** What does the gate's decision depend on?",
   "**RQ3.** Where does phishing get through once the LLM can clear a URL, and how stable is that error across sets?",
   "**RQ4.** Does sanitizing and framing page content reduce prompt-injection damage?"])
P("Our contributions are:")
L(["A recall-first cascade with a fail-closed rule, whose model choices were fixed on validation data and compared with eight baselines. It needs no network lookup before the LLM stage.",
   "A Vietnamese evaluation set of 4,265 URLs sharing no registrable domain with training data, with a label audit after which every experiment was rerun.",
   "A stratified analysis showing that the gate's clearing power and nearly all of its misses sit on Tranco-ranked domains, which the aggregate miss rate hides.",
   "A per-layer account of live errors, including a forward-in-time test on a same-day feed, showing that the LLM error rate ranges from 0 % to 16.6 % across sets, and a negative result for the domain re-registration explanation."], numbered=True)

H1("2   Related Work")
P("**Blocklists.** PhishTime measures how anti-phishing blacklists perform over time {c:phishtime}. We use public feeds as Layer A; they also supply training labels, which we treat as a threat to validity.")
P("**URL-based learning.** Classical work learns from features of the URL string {c:sahingoz}, while URLNet learns character and word representations with deep networks {c:urlnet}. PhiUSIIL adds a similarity index and a security profile {c:phiusiil}, and Zieni et al. survey website detection more broadly {c:zieni}. Layer C uses recurrent models {c:lstm,birnn} and gradient boosting {c:xgboost,lightgbm}.")
P("**Typosquatting.** Agten et al. studied typosquatting abuse over seven months {c:agten}. Layer B uses classic edit-distance and string-comparator measures {c:damerau,winkler}, applied to the whole URL and not only to the registered domain.")
P("**Page analysis with vision and LLMs.** Phishpedia identifies impersonated brands from page appearance {c:phishpedia}. ChatPhishDetector applies LLMs to phishing sites {c:chatphish}, and PhishAgent proposes a multimodal agent {c:phishagent}. We do not compare against these systems, which is a limitation of this work.")
P("**Prompt injection and benchmarks.** Greshake et al. showed indirect prompt injection against LLM-integrated applications {c:greshake}, the attack class Layer D faces. PhreshPhish provides a large phishing website benchmark {c:phreshphish}, which we use as an external test, and Tranco provides the popularity ranking {c:tranco}.")

H1("3   System Design")
P("Each URL passes through four layers ordered from cheap to expensive (Fig. 1). The design rule is asymmetric: an error, a timeout or missing evidence ends in a warning and never in a clean verdict.")
FIG(S.parent / "figures/fig1.png", 11.6, "**Fig. 1.** The cascade, with median latency per layer. Layer B passes a flag to later layers and cannot clear a URL.")
P("**Layer A** matches URLs against OpenPhish, PhishTank and Phishing.Database. A host-only feed entry does not block a popular or shared-platform root domain.")
P("**Layer B** compares keywords from the whole URL, including subdomain and path, with brand names, using Damerau-Levenshtein distance and Jaro-Winkler similarity. It flags 31 of 37 hand-written Vietnamese brand scenarios and 0.23 % of the Tranco top 100,000.")
P("**Layer C** clears a URL only when its score is below a threshold. Two cross-fitted character-level BiLSTM models produce an out-of-fold score, so that XGBoost does not train on over-confident in-sample scores. XGBoost then combines this score with lexical URL features, the local Tranco rank and an in-Tranco flag. Thresholds differ by segment: rank band (top 10,000, top 100,000, ranked, other) and URL shape (bare domain or path). Layer C performs no DNS, TLS or RDAP lookup. Those lookups cost 1 to 2.5 s for each new host, and removing them passed an acceptance rule, fixed before training, of at most 30 % more validation misses.")
P("**Layer D** fetches the page within 8 s. Hidden text, same-colour text, comments and invisible characters are removed, and injection patterns are filtered in 16 languages. Up to 2,500 characters of the remaining text are placed between random nonce markers and declared as data. Layer B flags and technical warnings enter the prompt, and the model answers phishing, suspicious or safe within a 12 s timeout.")
P("**Combining layers.** If Layer D says safe but the Layer C score is at least 0.99, the verdict becomes suspicious. If Layer D times out or the page cannot be read, fallback rules return phishing or suspicious. A hand-verified list of official Vietnamese brand domains is cleared directly unless a feed lists them. A DOM model was trained but stays off, since on validation it failed the rule of clearing at least 50 % of clean URLs.")

H2("3.1   Threat Model")
P("We consider an attacker who controls a URL and the content served at it, and who knows how the system works but cannot change its models or feeds. The attacker may register look-alike names, use free hosting or compromised domains, serve different content to different clients, and plant text aimed at the LLM. We do not consider attacks on the user's device, or on the detector's own infrastructure. A failure means a phishing URL ends with a clean verdict; a false warning is a cost, but not a failure.")
H2("3.2   Decision Logic")
P("Algorithm 1 gives the final routing. Two properties matter for the evaluation. First, only three paths end in clean: a verified official domain, a Layer C clearance without a Layer B flag, and a Layer D safe verdict that survives the override. Every miss in this paper comes from one of these. Second, a page that cannot be read never gets a clean verdict, which is why live results must be read together with the share of fallback warnings.")
CAP("**Algorithm 1.** Routing of a URL *u*.")
for line in ["1:  if A(u) lists u: return phishing  (feed entries on verified brand domains go on, and cannot be cleared)",
             "2:  if u is on a verified official domain: return clean",
             "3:  b ← B(u);  s ← C(u)",
             "4:  if b is not flagged and s < threshold(segment(u)): return clean",
             "5:  fetch the page; run A and B again on the final redirect target",
             "6:  if the target is listed: return phishing",
             "7:  if the page is unreadable or the LLM fails: return fallback(b, s)  (phishing or suspicious)",
             "8:  v ← D(sanitized page, b)",
             "9:  if v = safe and s ≥ 0.99: return suspicious",
             "10: return v"]:
    q = doc.add_paragraph(); q.paragraph_format.left_indent = Cm(1.0); q.paragraph_format.first_line_indent = Cm(-0.6)
    runs(q, line, 9)
sp = doc.add_paragraph(); sp.paragraph_format.space_after = Pt(4); first[0] = True

H1("4   Data")
P("We use one training corpus and several held-out sets that differ in source, language and date (Table 1). Phishing URLs age quickly, so every set is dated.")
CAP("**Table 1.** Datasets. The main corpus has 534,908 URLs: 374,699 train, 78,984 validation, 81,225 test.")
T([["Set", "Phishing", "Clean", "Source", "Date"],
   ["Main test split", "38,800", "42,425", "PhishTank, Phishing.Database, OpenPhish, PhiUSIIL; Tranco crawl", "built 2026-10-07"],
   ["Vietnamese", "2,681", "1,584", "ChongLuaDao; .vn sites from Tranco; verified brands", "listed 2024-05-16"],
   ["PhreshPhish sample", "9,991", "10,003", "test split, at most 3 URLs per host", "2025-09 to 2025-12"],
   ["Stored pages (D)", "1,004", "1,000", "PhreshPhish pages", "2025"],
   ["Live Vietnamese", "300", "300", "sampled from the Vietnamese set", "fetched 2026-10-23"],
   ["Fresh feed", "207", "200", "OpenPhish; deep links from Tranco top sites", "fetched 2026-11-04"]],
  [2.4, 1.4, 1.1, 4.4, 2.9], left_cols=1)
P("**Leakage controls.** Splits are made by site. The Vietnamese set shares no registrable domain with any training or evaluation file. PhreshPhish results are also reported on the subset of unseen domains (5,359 phishing, 7,317 clean).")
P("**Label audit.** Labels were corrected using evidence independent of Layer C, and all experiments were rerun afterwards. On the main corpus we dropped 2,703 rows whose domain was registered after the source dataset was collected, moved 188 Tranco-ranked homepages to clean when they were absent from current feeds and not reported by VirusTotal, and changed 60 more rows by hand review of 234 candidates. Hand review also changed 33 rows of the Vietnamese set and 11 of PhreshPhish. A 300-page subset checked against VirusTotal shows audited labels disagreeing on 6 of 148 decided rows (4.1 %, 95 % CI 1.9–8.6 %). This noise is far larger than the miss rates reported below, so differences of a few URLs between models mean little.")

H1("5   Experimental Setup")
P("All choices were made on validation or development data. The primary metric is the *miss rate*, the share of phishing URLs labelled clean. The cost metric is the *clearing rate*, the share of clean URLs cleared without the LLM. Model comparisons use one rule: the threshold is the lowest score that recognises at least 50 % of validation clean URLs. Selection covered five deep models (character CNN, BiLSTM, CNN-BiLSTM, URLNet, Transformer), then XGBoost, LightGBM and random forest on the winning score. The BiLSTM uses a 64-dimensional embedding, URLs cut to 200 characters, AdamW with a learning rate of 0.002, and up to 12 epochs with early stopping. XGBoost uses 2,000 trees of depth 8. Baselines have one seed; the ablation has three.")
P("Intervals are 95 % Wilson intervals {c:wilson}. The two LLMs are compared with paired exact McNemar tests {c:mcnemar}, and independent proportions with Fisher's exact test. Layer D uses gpt-6-luna unless stated, and claude-haiku-5-5 as a comparison. Both names are API model identifiers, queried in October 2026 for the stored runs and on 2026-11-04 for E1.")
P("Four analyses were added after the system was fixed, so none of them influenced a design choice:")
L(["**E0, reproduction.** The B+C and A+B+C routes were recomputed in the production Docker image from the frozen models.",
   "**E1, forward-in-time live test.** The OpenPhish feed fetched on 2026-11-04, 12 days after the models were trained, gave 296 URLs. With at most 2 URLs per domain this left 167 URLs on 142 domains unseen in training and 40 URLs on 28 domains seen in training. Every feed URL is listed, so Layer A was bypassed. The clean side is 200 fresh deep links from Tranco top sites, absent from the training data.",
   "**E2, stale-label check.** For the 300 phishing URLs of the live Vietnamese run, we compared the current RDAP registration date with the listing date. A later date means the domain was registered again after listing, so its label may no longer apply.",
   "**E3, stratification.** B+C results were split by whether the registrable domain has a Tranco rank."])

